Mixed-effects model repeated measures (MMRM) is a statistical analysis technique that can handle missing data by assuming that missing data would have followed the trend of the subject's treatment group. MMRM is used when a continuous response is measured repeatedly over time. 
MMRM can provide an unbiased estimate of the treatment effect that would have been observed if all patients had completed treatment. It does this by: Making use of all available data, Considering subject-specific effects, and Accounting for correlations between repeated measurements. 
MMRM is an acceptable approach for estimating the true treatment difference and controlling type I error rates when missing data is ignorable. However, there are many types of missing data scenarios, and it's possible that some of the data are not "missing at random" (MAR). 
Missing data can be caused by a number of factors, including: Patient refusal to continue, Withdrawal of consent, Adverse events that result in discontinuation of study treatment, and Poor record keeping. 
To reduce the amount of missing data, it's important to consider this early in the design stage of a study.
