How to create Table, Listing, and Figure (TLF) Shells: 

The Statistical Analysis Plan (SAP) and associated TLF shells are 1A and 1B when it comes to the deliverables the biostatistician produces during the study.

The SAP is the written language version of the statistician telling any reviewers of the clinical trial how the data was analyzed (what populations were used, what statistical techniques were implemented, how missing data was handled, etc.). 

The Shells then, are the physical representation of what the written language says. 

The Shells give the programming team something to program towards AND give the CSR development team (Sponsor, Medical Writer, Medical, etc.) an idea of what the final outputs will look like. 

Here is how to ensure you create shells that are clear and meaningful to everyone: 

1️⃣ Start with the SAP - as you write the SAP and any time you specify a table, figure, or listing will be generated, make a mark in the SAP for yourself. That mark is to tell you that you are calling for an output to be created. You can then create a table of contents for all the outputs the SAP is calling for. 

Remember, the SAP and Shells should be a 1:1 mapping exercise - if the SAP calls for an output, there had better be a shell, and if there is a shell, there had better be text in the SAP about that shell. 

2️⃣ Stand on the shoulders of giants - Very rarely have I created shells truly from scratch. I am more times than not creating shells from a template my company or the sponsor has, OR I am finding results/looking up other studies that have presented results similar to the type of results I plan on presenting - if you don't have a shell for what MMRM output should look like you can always go to clin trials dot gov and search for studies that have their statistical analysis plans or results available and see if there are any examples included (if not directly available from clintrials you can see if the drug was approved, and if it was, check out it's SBA on the FDA's website for the results and some inspiration). 

3️⃣ Take your time - Often (for one reason or another) you may find yourself with a tight timeline to create TLF shells. If you have any doubts that you do not have enough time to create the shells - speak with your manager as early as possible. The shells are CRITICAL for the success of the study analysis and if you rush through them you run the risk of the programming team getting stuck because the shells are not clear, or worse, you may end up creating a shell that cannot be used by the CSR development team. 

4️⃣ If ever you are not sure what to include in a shell - include as much as you can - even if it is overkill. It is much simpler to remove something than it is to add something later in the game. 

Shells are a huge topic and one that deserves more time to be covered - if you have any questions about creating them please feel free to send me a direct message.


