Phase 1 trials are conducted to assess the tolerability and pharmacologic properties of a new product; they are often called human pharmacology studies. Beginning with a single dose in a small number of subjects, additional trials will be conducted if the compound is judged to be adequately safe. Phase 1 is normally completed in 6–12 months and typically involves less than 100 participants, normally healthy volunteers. As Phase 1 progresses, more individuals are included and doses are increased to define the maximum tolerated dose. Researchers must pay special attention to the pharmacokinetic properties of the compound (ADME). Except when toxic compounds are being tested in patients with the indication under study (e.g., anti-cancer agents), Phase 1 trials do not attempt to assess the efficacy of the investigational product.
Phase 2 is conducted in patients with the condition being treated. It is used to assess the therapeutic benefit of the compound and to establish dose and trial design that will be used during Phase 3; it is often called the therapeutic exploratory phase. Generally, 100–200 participants will be included in multiple trials during a 1–2 year period. The number of participants studied during Phase 2 is not large enough to statistically verify the compound’s efficacy. The purpose of a Phase 2 trial is to balance the safety profile with the most effective dose in a target population. The results of this trial will determine the final dose to be tested in Phase 3 safety and efficacy trials.



Phases 3 and 4 are the longest phases to conduct. They are primarily interested in statistically confirming the efficacy of the treatment while continuing to assess its safety and tolerability. Phase 3 often compares current treatments to the new compound and is called the therapeutic confirmatory phase. Hundreds to thousands of subjects will be enrolled over a 2–3 year period. A marketing application is submitted at the end of Phase 3. 

Phase 4 clinical trials are conducted to identify and evaluate the long-term efficacy and safety of new drugs and treatments over a long period of time for a greater number of patients. Phase 4 research takes place after regulatory agency approval of a drug or device.




The sponsor must implement and maintain quality assurance and quality control systems with written standard operating procedures (SOPs) to ensure that clinical trials are conducted, documented, and reported in compliance with the protocol, GCP, and the applicable regulatory requirements reviewed by an Institutional Review Board (IRB)/Independent Ethics Committee (IEC). SOPs should be written for every aspect of new product development (e.g., selecting investigators, monitoring, product accountability, inspections). 

Quality control should be applied to each step of the data handling to ensure that all data are reliable and have been processed correctly. In addition, quality management should be part of every step of clinical trials, from design through conduct, recording, evaluation, reporting, and archiving.

Protocol Design

The sponsor must develop a sound investigational plan and a protocol to test a hypothesis while ensuring the rights, safety, and welfare of human subjects who volunteer to participate in the clinical trial. A protocol details the trial objectives, design, and organization. The protocol should also contain a clear description of investigators’ GCP responsibilities. 

The sponsor should utilize qualified individuals (e.g., biostatisticians, clinical pharmacologists, and physicians) as appropriate, throughout all stages of the clinical trial process including the protocol design. The protocol must be finalized and approved by an IRB/IEC prior to any human subject enrollment and/or informed consent.

Discontinuation of Trial

In the US, ClinicalTrials.gov must be updated to reflect the status of the trial. In the European Union, the sponsor is required to notify the competent authorities and ethics committees that the trial has ended. This information is sent through the EU portal. If a trial is terminated because of repeated noncompliance or fraud/misconduct by an investigator, sponsors are obligated to end the investigator’s participation and notify the applicable regulatory authorities. It is also appropriate for the sponsor to directly notify the governing IRB/IEC immediately.

Sponsor/CRO Collaboration 

A sponsor may transfer any or all trial-related duties and functions to a Contract Research Organization (CRO). However, the ultimate responsibility for the quality and integrity of the trial data remains with the sponsor. Any sponsor obligation transferred to a CRO must be documented in writing. It is acceptable to simply state that all responsibilities have been transferred or specify the responsibilities that have been transferred to a CRO, if this is the case. Most sponsors and CROs have standard “Transfer-of-Regulatory-Obligations” templates. Remember, if it is not documented, the responsibility has not been transferred. 

The sponsor and the CRO must document what SOPs are being followed.
Sponsors design case report forms (CRFs) to capture all safety and efficacy data required by the protocol. CRFs are paper or electronic documents designed to record all protocol-required information reported to the sponsor on each trial subject.

Sponsors should provide guidance to investigators and/or the investigators’ designated representatives on making corrections to CRFs. Sponsors should also have written procedures to ensure that changes or corrections in CRFs made by sponsors and sponsors’ designated representatives are documented, are necessary, and are endorsed by the principal investigator.

The sponsor is responsible for developing and updating an investigator’s brochure (IB). An IB is a compilation of the nonclinical and clinical data on the investigational product. The IB provides investigators and others involved in the clinical trial with the information needed for understanding and compliance with the protocol.  

The IB should present information in a concise, simple, objective, balanced, and nonpromotional form. The IB should be reviewed at least annually and revised as necessary in compliance with a sponsor’s written procedures. More frequent revisions may be appropriate depending on the state of development and the generation of relevant new information. It must enable a potential investigator to understand and make an assessment of the appropriateness to administer the product in the proposed trial.

The IB should be updated in accordance with GCP. Relevant new information may be so important that it should be communicated to the investigators, and possibly to the IRB/IEC and/or regulatory authorities before it is included in a revised IB.

E6 (R2) describes the content of the IB under section 7 with an example provided [7.3, 7.4]. 21 CFR 312.23 describes specific FDA IB requirements.

Sponsors must select appropriately qualified individuals to supervise the overall conduct of the trial. Monitors must be qualified by training and experience and possess the scientific and/or clinical knowledge required to monitor the trial adequately. Sponsors should document the qualifications of a monitor.

Sponsors should ensure that monitors are thoroughly familiar with the investigational product and have a complete understanding of the IB, protocol, CRF, and the informed consent form. Sponsors should confirm that monitors understand and will follow SOPs, have in-depth knowledge of GCP and applicable regulatory requirements, and are able to ensure that investigators and their staff follow GCP.

A clinical trial is any investigation in research subjects intended to discover or verify the clinical, pharmacological, and/or other pharmacodynamic effects of an investigational product, and/or to identify any adverse reactions to an investigational product(s), and/or to absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion of an investigational product(s) with the object of ascertaining its safety and/or efficacy. The terms clinical trial and clinical study are synonymous.

CROs: Many of the sponsor’s duties and functions may be transferred to a Contract Research Organization (CRO); however, the ultimate responsibility for the quality and integrity of trial data remains with the sponsor.

Responsibilities of a sponsor include: 

ensuring a proper IRB/IEC is identified.
ensuring sufficient data is available to support human experimentation.
implementing and maintaining QA and QC systems.

Responsibilities of a monitor include: 

• conducting frequent visits of investigational sites. 
• verifying that investigators have adequate resources to conduct a trial and are qualified.
• asking questions diplomatically to clarify information.

Responsibilities of an investigator include: 

submitting reports and conduct follow-ups on adverse events.
complying with all requirements for obtaining informed consent.
ensuring the research subjects have adequate medical care.
